trim -- rewrites a permutation in its smallest representation
*************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        trim w
  * Inputs:
      * w, a *note permutation: Permutation,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Strategy: (Macaulay2Doc)Complement, => ..., default value null, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note permutation: Permutation,, 

Description
===========

trim rewrites a permutation $p$ as a permutation in $S_n$, where $n$ is the
smallest integer such that $p$ is in $\mathfrak{S}_n$. In other words, it
returns a permutation where any extraneous fixed points are removed.

+-------------------------------------+
|i1 : p = permutation {3,1,2,5,4,6,7} |
|                                     |
|o1 = Permutation{3, 1, 2, 5, 4, 6, 7}|
|                                     |
|o1 : Permutation                     |
+-------------------------------------+
|i2 : trim p                          |
|                                     |
|o2 = Permutation{3, 1, 2, 5, 4}      |
|                                     |
|o2 : Permutation                     |
+-------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note extend: (Macaulay2Doc)extend, -- extend an object

Ways to use trim:
=================

  * "trim(Ideal)" -- see *note trim: (Macaulay2Doc)trim, -- minimize generators
    and relations
